WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.200
During the Hundred Years’ War,&nbsp;
France faced a formidable opponent:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:17.680
the invading armies of England. The English relied&nbsp;
on a distinctive tactical system that combined the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:24.240
devastating firepower of their longbowmen with&nbsp;
the steadfastness of heavily armed men-at-arms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:29.120
Time and again, even numerically superior&nbsp;
French armies struggled against this deadly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:34.560
combination. Their commanders desperately&nbsp;
searched for a way to counter the English&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:40.560
tactics—and paid dearly for every attempt.
In this video, we’ll explore eight of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:44.720
the most significant battles from the&nbsp;
early stages of the Hundred Years’ War.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:50.080
We’ll see how the English system worked in&nbsp;
practice, how the French tried to counter it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:54.160
and how they eventually discovered a way&nbsp;
to hold their own against the invaders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.160 --> 00:01:06.400
from across the Channel.
English Tactics in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.240
Hundred Years’ War—and How to Counter Them
The Hundred Years’ War began in 1337.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:15.760
Not long after, Edward III of England pressed his&nbsp;
claim to the French throne and crossed the Channel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:23.360
to assert his rights. Fully aware that France,&nbsp;
the economic and military powerhouse of its time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:28.880
could field larger armies and that he would be at&nbsp;
a disadvantage in a drawn-out war of attrition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:35.600
Edward sought to force the French into a decisive&nbsp;
battle right from the beginning. He was confident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:39.975
that his troops could prevail against the armies&nbsp;
of heavily armed French knights, the military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.975 --> 00:01:43.520
super power of the time, in the open field,&nbsp;
because England had developed a very effective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:52.560
tactical system in its wars against the Scots.
After a spectacularly failed cavalry charge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.040
against Scottish infantry at the&nbsp;
Battle of Bannockburn in 1314,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:04.160
the English adapted their tactics. They&nbsp;
now relied on a combination of dismounted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:09.040
men-at-arms and longbowmen—a combination that&nbsp;
proved its effectiveness in the Battles of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:17.520
Dupplin Moor in 1332 and Halidon Hill in 1333.
At Halidon Hill, the English took a strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:22.720
position atop a hill, forcing the Scots to&nbsp;
attack across marshy ground, up the slope,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:28.880
and through a storm of longbowmen’s arrows. When&nbsp;
the exhausted and battered attackers reached the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:33.520
English line, the dismounted men-at-arms blocked&nbsp;
their advance while the archers caught them in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:39.840
a deadly crossfire. The battle was brief&nbsp;
but brutal. When the Scots broke and fled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:51.200
the men-at-arms remounted and chased them off the&nbsp;
field, crushing the last remnants of resistance.&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:59.200
By the time the English arrived in France, news&nbsp;
of the Scots’ defeats had reached Philip VI. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:04.480
knew just how effective English tactics could&nbsp;
be, and despite Edward’s efforts, he avoided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:11.680
open battle—even when the two kings met in the&nbsp;
field in 1339. Edward took a strong defensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:17.120
position on a hill between the villages of La&nbsp;
Capelle and La Flamengerie, securing the front&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:23.280
of his army with a ditch and field fortifications.&nbsp;
Behind these defenses, the dismounted men-at-arms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:29.280
formed three solid blocks, each flanked by&nbsp;
archers. This shows how crucial the careful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:34.240
selection of a strong defensive position&nbsp;
was to the success of the English tactics.&nbsp;

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:40.080
At La Flamengerie, however, it became clear that&nbsp;
a position could be so strong that it became a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:46.000
deterrent. When Philip’s scouts reported the&nbsp;
English deployment, he abandoned the attack and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.360
instead dug in behind a line of abatis—field&nbsp;
fortifications made of sharpened tree trunks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:57.440
and branches. His men saw this as cowardly, but&nbsp;
though Philip’s caution clashed with the French&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:03.680
knights’ ideas of honor, it spared France&nbsp;
a potentially disastrous defeat at the very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:09.280
start of the war. Philip maintained this cautious&nbsp;
approach, which meant that the first major field&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:14.880
battle of the conflict would not be between the&nbsp;
kings themselves, but between their deputies in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:29.520
the War of the Breton Succession.
The Battle of Morlaix 1342&nbsp;

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:37.200
Between 1341 and 1345, several truces interrupted&nbsp;
the war between England and France. During this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:43.840
period, the focus shifted to the Breton War of&nbsp;
Succession, which became a kind of proxy conflict.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:50.800
Two men were contenders to succeed John III,&nbsp;
the recently deceased Duke of Brittany: John of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:58.960
Montfort, John III’s half-brother, and Charles of&nbsp;
Blois, the husband of John’s niece and Philip VI’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:08.480
nephew. Naturally, Philip VI supported Charles of&nbsp;
Blois, while Edward III backed John of Montfort.&nbsp;

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:15.280
To support his candidate, Edward sent William&nbsp;
de Bohun, 1st Earl of Northampton, to Brittany.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:20.160
He landed at Brest in the summer of&nbsp;
1342, liberated the besieged city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:26.080
and set out to capture the fortress of Morlaix.&nbsp;
Charles of Blois, who had initially retreated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:31.920
soon realized he had more than twice the&nbsp;
strength of his opponents and gave chase.&nbsp;

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:36.960
When the Earl of Northampton learned that the&nbsp;
Franco-Breton army was approaching, he lifted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:43.360
the siege and took up a position at the edge of&nbsp;
a grove. His force of nearly 5,000 men fortified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:50.480
the area with traps cleverly camouflaged with hay&nbsp;
and undergrowth. The exact formations and course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:55.960
of the battle are unclear, but chroniclers report&nbsp;
that the English men-at-arms fought dismounted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.960 --> 00:06:01.360
the French first attacked Blois’s knights&nbsp;
on horseback—likely on the flanks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:07.520
where the archers inflicted heavy losses—and then&nbsp;
they pressed head-on against the center, where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:13.360
traps took their toll. Despite these obstacles,&nbsp;
the French continued their advance and clashed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:18.960
with the English line in a brutal hand-to-hand&nbsp;
fight. After several hours of fierce combat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:24.800
the Earl of Northampton emerged victorious.
The Battle of Morlaix not only confirmed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:30.480
importance of carefully selecting and preparing&nbsp;
a strong defensive position but also demonstrated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:36.480
that the men-at-arms were the backbone of English&nbsp;
formations. No matter how relentless the hail of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:42.560
arrows from the archers, the enemy almost always&nbsp;
reached the English front line. It was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:48.240
steadfast men-at-arms who decided the battle’s&nbsp;
outcome. Without this human wall, the lightly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:54.400
armed archers would have had little chance against&nbsp;
a direct assault. So, the English tactics relied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.400 --> 00:07:00.640
on a kind of symbiosis: the archers weakened the&nbsp;
enemy before they reached the men-at-arms, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:08.160
men-at-arms stopped the attackers, shielded the&nbsp;
archers, and ultimately finished the enemy off.&nbsp;

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:14.560
At Morlaix, English tactics proved their worth on&nbsp;
French soil for the first time. Charles of Blois,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:20.160
one of France’s most capable commanders, took the&nbsp;
lesson to heart and immediately began searching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:25.120
for a way to counter the English system. The&nbsp;
results of his efforts became clear four years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:41.680
later at the Battle of Saint-Pol-de-Léon.
The Battle of Saint-Pol-de-Léon 1346&nbsp;

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:48.480
In 1345, Edward III broke the truce with France,&nbsp;
which was supposed to last until the summer of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:54.880
1346. He sent troops to Brittany to repel Charles&nbsp;
of Blois and secure a potential bridgehead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.880 --> 00:08:01.120
to land his next invasion army. After initial&nbsp;
successes, the campaign stalled, as the English&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:09.920
failed to capture a port in northern Brittany.
By 1346, Edward had decided to land in Normandy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:18.240
with his main army. Still, Sir Thomas Dagworth&nbsp;
advanced west, likely on a reconnaissance mission,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:25.200
and was confronted by Charles of Blois at the&nbsp;
small town of Saint-Pol-de-Léon. Dagworth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:32.240
commanding just 80 men-at-arms and 120 mounted&nbsp;
archers, was heavily outnumbered by Blois’s 1,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:38.880
men. With no chance to retreat, he had his troops&nbsp;
dismount and take up a strong position on a hill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:45.920
securing the front with a ditch and the rear&nbsp;
with an improvised barrier of wagons and horses.&nbsp;

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:51.280
Having learned from Morlaix that cavalry was&nbsp;
highly vulnerable in a frontal assault on an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:56.560
English formation, Charles of Blois dismounted&nbsp;
the majority of his men-at-arms. This made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:02.960
Saint-Pol-de-Léon one of the first battles&nbsp;
in which French men-at-arms fought on foot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:08.320
Aside from reduced mobility, there were few&nbsp;
drawbacks: the men were just as well-trained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:13.120
for fighting on foot as on horseback, and&nbsp;
their close-combat skills had been honed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:19.120
in the many small sieges of the early Hundred&nbsp;
Years’ War. To offset the loss of mobility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:25.600
Charles kept about 40 heavily armored horsemen and&nbsp;
a division of crossbowmen in reserve at the rear.&nbsp;

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:31.600
The Franco-Breton army attacked from two sides&nbsp;
simultaneously. Charles sent his mobile striking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:37.200
force around Dagworth’s flank to strike from the&nbsp;
rear, while the dismounted men-at-arms assaulted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:42.560
the English front in waves. The flanking&nbsp;
maneuver stalled at the English wagon line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:48.400
and the archers unleashed a devastating hail of&nbsp;
arrows on the attackers approaching the front.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:53.520
Despite these setbacks, the Franco-Breton&nbsp;
forces pressed forward, and a fierce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.520 --> 00:10:00.000
daylong battle ensued. In the late afternoon,&nbsp;
after his men were beaten back multiple times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:06.560
Charles personally led a final assault. Against&nbsp;
the backdrop of the setting sun, he attacked the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:13.920
English from all sides with three divisions,&nbsp;
but was repelled once more. Saint-Pol-de-Léon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:18.640
highlighted the deadly effectiveness of the&nbsp;
English combination of dismounted men-at-arms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:25.200
field fortifications and longbowmen. Though&nbsp;
outnumbered roughly five to one, not a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:31.360
English man-at-arms fell, while Charles&nbsp;
of Blois lost a large portion of his army.&nbsp;

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:36.960
Even though Charles had lost at Saint-Pol-de-Léon,&nbsp;
French commanders recognized that dismounted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:41.520
men-at-arms combined with a mobile assault&nbsp;
force offered the most promising counter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:47.840
to English tactics so far. Nevertheless,&nbsp;
this approach would be put to the test when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:59.200
the first major field battle of the Hundred&nbsp;
Years’ War took place just a few weeks later.&nbsp;

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:04.480
Quick book recommendation before we go on:&nbsp;
Bernard Cornwell’s Grail Quest is a fantastic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:09.040
well-researched historical-fiction series that&nbsp;
brings the battles of the early Hundred Years’ War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:13.360
to life. If you want to check it out, there’s an&nbsp;
Amazon link below. Buying through that link gives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:18.320
us a small commission at no extra cost to you&nbsp;
and really helps the channel. Prefer audiobooks?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:22.720
You can also grab it on Audible through our&nbsp;
link and get a free audiobook. All links are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:31.680
in the description and pinned comment. Thanks&nbsp;
for the considering it—now back to the video.&nbsp;

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:37.680
The Battle of Crécy 1346
When Edward III landed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:43.440
Normandy with his invading army in July&nbsp;
1346 and began marching through France,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:49.360
plundering as he went, Philip VI had&nbsp;
little choice but to confront him. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:54.960
pursued the English north from Poissy near&nbsp;
Paris, tried to block them at the Somme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.960 --> 00:12:01.440
and ultimately near the small town of Crécy—“fell&nbsp;
directly into the King of England's trap,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:07.200
as historian Matthew Strickland puts it.
The exact deployment of the English and many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:12.960
other details of the Battle of Crécy remain hotly&nbsp;
debated. This is not the place to go into those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:18.240
debates, especially since the battle represents&nbsp;
a special case in terms of the French approach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:23.440
as we will see. What is certain is that the&nbsp;
English took up a strong defensive position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.440 --> 00:12:29.440
on a hill between the villages of Crécy and&nbsp;
Wadicourt. As had become standard practice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:34.640
they secured the rear with wagons and prepared&nbsp;
traps and field fortifications in front of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:41.120
line. The dismounted men-at-arms formed&nbsp;
three divisions, each flanked by archers.&nbsp;

00:12:41.120 --> 00:12:47.440
The head of the French column reached the&nbsp;
battlefield shortly before midday. Despite the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:54.080
strong English position, Philip VI attacked before&nbsp;
his troops were fully assembled. The reason is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.080 --> 00:13:00.640
unclear—some suggest political pressure compelled&nbsp;
him to act hastily, while others say his knights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:05.840
urged immediate action. Whatever the reason,&nbsp;
Philip sent the Genoese crossbowmen forward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:12.080
first—and instantly made a critical mistake.&nbsp;
His mercenaries’ equipment had been left at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.080 --> 00:13:17.360
the rear of the French column, forcing them&nbsp;
to attack with reduced armor and ammunition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:24.240
and—most importantly—without the protective&nbsp;
pavises central to their tactics. Vulnerable and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:31.280
exposed, they were quickly forced to fall back.
The second wave, composed of mounted men-at-arms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:37.280
pressed forward ruthlessly, riding over the&nbsp;
retreating crossbowmen. This took away much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:43.200
of their momentum, and they lost cohesion just as&nbsp;
they entered the range of the longbowmen. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:47.920
the French men-at-arms reached the English&nbsp;
right wing, commanded by Edward of Woodstock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:54.720
the Black Prince. Just as Edward III had intended,&nbsp;
the terrain and English formation funneled the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.720 --> 00:14:01.200
French into a death zone in front of the prince’s&nbsp;
division. His men acted as a buffer, wearing down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:07.760
wave after wave of French attackers. The assault&nbsp;
only ended around midnight, and the next morning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.760 --> 00:14:13.040
the English men-at-arms mounted their horses&nbsp;
and swept the remainder of Philip’s army from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:18.560
the battlefield, completing one of the most&nbsp;
one-sided victories of the Hundred Years’ War.&nbsp;

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:26.880
Despite his caution, in the end, Philip VI did&nbsp;
engage in battle—and paid a heavy price. Crécy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.880 --> 00:14:32.800
is often seen as a triumph of the longbow over&nbsp;
French men-at-arms, highlighting flaws in French&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.800 --> 00:14:39.600
tactics and organization. In fact, it was not.&nbsp;
The defeat had more to do with Philip’s hasty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:45.360
approach. His attack was poorly coordinated,&nbsp;
and he ignored the lessons Charles of Blois had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:52.800
learned during the War of the Breton Succession.&nbsp;
Crécy was, in the words of Matthew Strickland, “a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:59.120
disastrous shambles unrepresentative of the real&nbsp;
capabilities of French arms or tactical thinking.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.320 --> 00:15:05.680
In several smaller battles over the following&nbsp;
years, the French proved they were capable of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:20.531
far more than they had shown at Crécy.
Lunalonge 1349, Taillebourg 1351,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.531 --> 00:15:23.600
Ardres 1351, and Mauron 1352
In 1349, Thomas Coke, the English&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:28.480
seneschal of Gascony, was ambushed&nbsp;
near the small village of Lunalonge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:32.960
He immediately ordered his men-at-arms and&nbsp;
archers to dismount and take up a strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.960 --> 00:15:39.520
position on a hill. The French, led by Jean de&nbsp;
Lille and outnumbering them roughly three to one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:45.600
attacked confidently. One division rode around&nbsp;
the Anglo-Gascon troops to strike at their rear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:51.600
while two others assaulted from the front.
The troops that bypassed the English managed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:57.440
to seize some horses and baggage, but the&nbsp;
frontal attackers met fierce resistance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:03.120
The horses making it through the hail of arrows&nbsp;
were mercilessly impaled on the spears of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.120 --> 00:16:09.280
dismounted men-at-arms. By evening, when Jean&nbsp;
de Lille called off the attack, 300 of his men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.280 --> 00:16:16.160
had fallen. The battle confirmed once and for all&nbsp;
that frontal cavalry charges against well-deployed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:21.680
English troops were virtually suicidal.
The combination of dismounted men-at-arms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:26.560
and mounted shock troops now quickly spread&nbsp;
from Brittany into the rest of France.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:37.280
In 1351, when Marshal Guy II de Nesle&nbsp;
besieged the fortress of Saint-Jean-d’Angély,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.280 --> 00:16:43.600
he intercepted a convoy of reinforcements at a&nbsp;
bridge over the Charente River near Taillebourg.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:48.480
Following Charles of Blois’s example, the&nbsp;
marshal dismounted the bulk of his men-at-arms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:54.720
only keeping two cavalry reserves on the wings.&nbsp;
He then prepared to attack both frontally and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.720 --> 00:17:01.120
from the flanks. However, the plan never came&nbsp;
to fruition: reinforcements from Taillebourg&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:07.200
and Tonnay-Charente arrived from the rear,&nbsp;
surprising Guy de Nesle before he could advance.&nbsp;

00:17:07.840 --> 00:17:15.440
Two weeks later, on June 6, 1351, Édouard I de&nbsp;
Beaujeu attacked an English raiding party led&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.440 --> 00:17:23.840
by John de Beauchamp, the captain of Calais. Near&nbsp;
Ardres, about 600 kilometers north of Taillebourg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.840 --> 00:17:28.720
the retreating English took up a defensive&nbsp;
position in a bend of the river and secured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.720 --> 00:17:34.560
it with a ditch. Once again, the French attacked&nbsp;
using a combination of dismounted men-at-arms and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:40.880
mounted shock troops. Although Édouard de Beaujeu&nbsp;
was killed at the very start of the battle, his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.880 --> 00:17:46.720
men kept up the pressure until the English archers&nbsp;
ran out of arrows. This allowed the French cavalry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.720 --> 00:17:52.640
to bypass the defenders and strike from the rear.&nbsp;
While Ardres cannot be considered a decisive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.640 --> 00:17:57.840
success for the French method—since it was likely&nbsp;
the lack of arrow reserves that decided the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.840 --> 00:18:11.360
outcome—combined with Taillebourg, it demonstrates&nbsp;
that the tactic had now spread throughout France.&nbsp;

00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:18.000
How promising the French approach was became&nbsp;
clear in the Battle of Mauron in 1352. This time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:23.920
Guy de Nesle faced an Anglo-Breton force under&nbsp;
Sir Walter Bentley. Bentley had positioned his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.920 --> 00:18:28.560
men on a slight rise between a hedge&nbsp;
and some bushes. As was now standard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.560 --> 00:18:36.240
about 1,500 men-at-arms formed the center, while&nbsp;
roughly 800 archers held the wings. Again, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:36.240 --> 00:18:42.160
French attacked both frontally and on the flanks.&nbsp;
This time, the assault force broke through at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.160 --> 00:18:48.800
first attempt—with devastating results. When the&nbsp;
armored horsemen smashed into the unprotected side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.800 --> 00:18:55.520
of the English right wing, it collapsed almost&nbsp;
immediately, exposing the center. Meanwhile, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.520 --> 00:19:00.400
French men-at-arms fought their way through the&nbsp;
bushes in front of the English position, attacking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.400 --> 00:19:07.120
head-on and pressing the enemy against the hedge.&nbsp;
Victory seemed within reach—until the French&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.120 --> 00:19:13.840
center suddenly faltered. On the right, part of&nbsp;
the formation inexplicably fell back, giving the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.840 --> 00:19:19.440
English archers an opportunity to advance and&nbsp;
strike into the exposed flank of the French.&nbsp;

00:19:19.440 --> 00:19:25.840
At Mauron, the French came closer to defeating&nbsp;
an English formation than at any point since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.840 --> 00:19:32.160
the start of the war. Their plan had worked&nbsp;
flawlessly—until the inexplicable retreat of part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.160 --> 00:19:38.960
of their men-at-arms disrupted the attack. This&nbsp;
near-success, however, helped shape French tactics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.960 --> 00:19:45.440
for the next major engagement. At Poitiers,&nbsp;
the combination of dismounted men-at-arms and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.440 --> 00:20:03.440
fast-moving shock troops would be deployed in a&nbsp;
large-scale battle for the first time in the war.&nbsp;

00:20:03.440 --> 00:20:07.440
The Battle of Poitiers 1356
In 1356, the Black Prince led a chevauchée&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:14.480
from Gascony north into Poitou. King John II&nbsp;
pursued him and confronted the English south&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:14.480 --> 00:20:22.160
of Poitiers. Prince Edward, seizing a convenient&nbsp;
opportunity for battle, chose a position perfectly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.160 --> 00:20:28.800
suited to English tactics. A gentle slope with&nbsp;
a few hedgerows lay in front of his troops,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.800 --> 00:20:35.600
the forest of Nouaillé behind them, and the&nbsp;
Miosson River to their left. On the right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.600 --> 00:20:40.880
the English built a barricade of wagons and&nbsp;
brushwood. Edward deployed his forces in three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.880 --> 00:20:47.120
divisions, with longbowmen forming the wings and&nbsp;
securing the front. They dug trenches between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.120 --> 00:20:52.640
hedgerows and constructed field fortifications&nbsp;
to strengthen their position. Behind the army,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.640 --> 00:20:58.480
a mounted reserve stood ready to exploit&nbsp;
any breakthrough or launch a counterattack.&nbsp;

00:20:58.480 --> 00:21:05.520
On September 18, as the two armies faced each&nbsp;
other, King John II drew up his plan of attack. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:12.800
was determined to avoid repeating the disaster of&nbsp;
Crécy. His troops had thoroughly reconnoitered the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.800 --> 00:21:19.840
English position, were well-rested, and the king&nbsp;
had carefully considered how he wanted to proceed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:19.840 --> 00:21:25.200
He divided his forces—approximately&nbsp;
10,000 men-at-arms, 2,000 infantry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:25.200 --> 00:21:31.360
and 2,000 crossbowmen—into three divisions. Each&nbsp;
division consisted of a central body of foot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.360 --> 00:21:38.800
soldiers flanked by crossbowmen. Aside from two&nbsp;
smaller shock troops totaling 300 to 500 armored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.800 --> 00:21:45.600
horsemen, all the men-at-arms fought on foot.
On the morning of September 19, Edward prepared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.600 --> 00:21:52.960
for battle, but then unexpectedly ordered&nbsp;
his left wing to retreat across the Miosson.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.960 --> 00:21:57.680
The French feared this might be a feint&nbsp;
designed to lure them into attacking an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.680 --> 00:22:03.920
almost impregnable position, but they decided&nbsp;
to take the chance and press the assault.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:03.920 --> 00:22:08.880
One group of mounted men skirted the hedge on&nbsp;
the right, aiming to strike the side of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.880 --> 00:22:13.920
exposed English center, while another tried&nbsp;
to force a gap through the bushes to attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.920 --> 00:22:19.680
the English right wing. When the first group&nbsp;
reached the hedge, English archers caught them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:19.680 --> 00:22:27.920
in a deadly crossfire. Arrows fell like rain,&nbsp;
bringing down horses and routing their riders.&nbsp;

00:22:27.920 --> 00:22:33.200
Meanwhile, the second assault force had nearly&nbsp;
reached the left flank of the English center.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.200 --> 00:22:38.880
Just as the riders accelerated to charge,&nbsp;
the English left wing appeared behind them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.880 --> 00:22:45.280
having returned from the ford and launching a&nbsp;
counterattack. Caught between two fronts and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:45.280 --> 00:22:50.560
under heavy fire, the French men-at-arms&nbsp;
could not hold their ground for long.&nbsp;

00:22:50.560 --> 00:22:56.800
With the rapid mounted assault having failed,&nbsp;
the first French division on foot advanced. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.800 --> 00:23:02.400
they pushed through the hedgerows, they suffered&nbsp;
heavy losses at the hands of the English archers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:02.400 --> 00:23:08.160
Nevertheless, the men reached the English&nbsp;
front, and a bloody hand-to-hand battle ensued.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:08.160 --> 00:23:12.720
Despite fierce fighting, the English&nbsp;
line held firm, and after two hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.720 --> 00:23:18.000
the French were forced to retreat.
So far, all of King John’s attacks had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:24.880
failed. However, two-thirds of his army was still&nbsp;
fresh, while the English were exhausted. Many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:24.880 --> 00:23:31.120
were wounded, and their archers were running&nbsp;
low on arrows. Yet the French, once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:31.120 --> 00:23:37.040
made a critical mistake. The second division&nbsp;
interpreted the retreat of the first wave as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:37.040 --> 00:23:43.360
a signal to pull back, abandoning the advance&nbsp;
instead of maintaining pressure. This pause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.360 --> 00:23:47.760
gave Edward time to regroup his troops&nbsp;
before King John’s third division could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:47.760 --> 00:23:55.280
engage. France had squandered another opportunity.
When King John attacked with the third division,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:55.280 --> 00:24:00.160
he sent his crossbowmen ahead.&nbsp;
Unlike the mercenaries at Crécy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:00.160 --> 00:24:06.320
these troops were fully equipped. Step by step,&nbsp;
they advanced up the slope with their pavises,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:06.320 --> 00:24:13.680
securing the ground to the English front. Slowly&nbsp;
but surely, Edward’s situation became precarious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:13.680 --> 00:24:19.360
Many of his archers had already set aside&nbsp;
their bows—useless without arrows—and drawn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:19.360 --> 00:24:26.560
their swords to engage in close combat. Yet the&nbsp;
Black Prince still had one last ace up his sleeve.&nbsp;

00:24:27.520 --> 00:24:33.680
Around 60 men-at-arms, accompanied by a few dozen&nbsp;
mounted archers, had ridden in a wide arc around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:33.680 --> 00:24:40.320
a hill to the right of the battlefield. Now, they&nbsp;
charged into the rear of King John’s forces. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:40.320 --> 00:24:45.840
surprise attack proved a turning point. As&nbsp;
the French scrambled to deal with the threat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:45.840 --> 00:24:50.960
behind them, the Black Prince ordered some of his&nbsp;
men-at-arms to remount and strike the shattered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:50.960 --> 00:24:57.040
formation from the front and flank. The English&nbsp;
horsemen thundered down the hill, crashing into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:57.040 --> 00:25:03.120
the French lines. John’s division disintegrated&nbsp;
under the force of the impact and soon collapsed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:03.120 --> 00:25:11.280
completely, sealing the outcome of the battle.
The French had suffered another crushing defeat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:11.280 --> 00:25:17.440
Around 2,500 of their men-at-arms were&nbsp;
killed, and 3,000 men—including King John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:17.440 --> 00:25:25.280
himself—were taken prisoner. The English,&nbsp;
by contrast, lost just 40 men-at-arms.&nbsp;

00:25:25.280 --> 00:25:31.280
Yet Poitiers also showed that the English&nbsp;
method was no longer infallible. A full third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:31.280 --> 00:25:37.440
of the French army had never struck a blow,&nbsp;
and still the victory was far harder won than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:37.440 --> 00:25:44.000
most English triumphs of the previous 20 years.&nbsp;
In earlier battles, the first volleys of arrows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:44.000 --> 00:25:54.480
often decided the outcome—but at Poitiers, the&nbsp;
result remained uncertain even after 100 volleys.&nbsp;

00:25:54.480 --> 00:26:00.480
When the first phase of the Hundred Years’&nbsp;
War ended with the Peace of Brétigny in 1360,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:00.480 --> 00:26:06.240
the French had still not found a reliable way&nbsp;
to counter the English tactical system. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:06.240 --> 00:26:12.000
their adaptations—both tactical and technical—had&nbsp;
made them far better able to withstand the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:12.000 --> 00:26:17.360
firepower of the English longbowmen. Dismounted&nbsp;
men-at-arms were less vulnerable to arrows than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:17.360 --> 00:26:23.680
mounted ones, and heavier armor and the increasing&nbsp;
use of pavises gave them even more protection.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:23.680 --> 00:26:29.920
At the same time, mounted shock troops provided a&nbsp;
mobile element for flanking attacks. But although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:29.920 --> 00:26:35.040
these changes reduced the effectiveness of the&nbsp;
English longbowmen against French men-at-arms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:35.040 --> 00:26:42.480
they were still not enough to overcome&nbsp;
the full strength of English tactics.&nbsp;

00:26:42.480 --> 00:26:49.440
It was only when the war resumed in 1369 that the&nbsp;
French finally found a way to counter the English&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:49.440 --> 00:26:56.080
system. After the Battle of Poitiers, Charles&nbsp;
V assumed control of the government while his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:56.080 --> 00:27:01.680
father remained a prisoner of the English. He&nbsp;
realized the futility of continuing to counter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:01.680 --> 00:27:07.600
the English tactics with direct confrontation, as&nbsp;
it was not the most effective way to defeat them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:07.600 --> 00:27:13.040
The real underlying problem was that the English&nbsp;
could plunder, raid, and besiege French territory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:13.040 --> 00:27:19.280
unhindered if they were not stopped. While&nbsp;
battles were the usual means to prevent this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:19.280 --> 00:27:26.560
they were not the only option. Instead, Charles&nbsp;
V adopted a Fabian strategy, meaning he avoided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:26.560 --> 00:27:33.760
major engagements, instead relying on attrition&nbsp;
and indirect attacks. In sieges and small-scale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:33.760 --> 00:27:40.400
skirmishes, the French were easily able to&nbsp;
keep pace with the English, and by 1374 they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:40.400 --> 00:27:45.920
had recaptured nearly everything they had been&nbsp;
forced to cede under the Peace of Brétigny.&nbsp;

00:27:45.920 --> 00:27:52.240
Yet Charles and his father recognized that lasting&nbsp;
victory would require additional measures. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:52.240 --> 00:27:58.640
1363, they began reforming the French armies,&nbsp;
creating a modern war machine of their own.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:58.640 --> 00:28:05.600
How these new powerful armies dealt with the&nbsp;
English, however, is a topic for another video.

